# libetpan in rampart — a subset, not the upstream tree

This directory is NOT a copy of libetpan. It is the RFC 5322 + MIME parsing
subset, extracted deliberately, plus one file of ours. If you go looking for
`libetpan.h`, IMAP, SMTP, POP3, NNTP, SSL, mailstream or the storage drivers,
they are not here and were never meant to be.

Upstream: https://github.com/dinhvh/libetpan
License:  BSD 3-Clause — see COPYRIGHT.
Taken from master at commit dated 2026-08-22.

## Why a subset

rampart-totext needs a MIME parser. libetpan is a mail *framework*: the parser
is about 26,000 lines of a much larger tree whose remainder is network
protocols we have no use for. The imf/mime layers turn out to depend on
nothing but three container types, so the cut is clean — verified by
compiling these files standalone with no other part of upstream present.

## What is here

    src/mailimf*.c            RFC 5322 header parsing
    src/mailmime*.c           MIME structure, RFC 2047 encoded-words,
                              RFC 2231 parameters, Content-Disposition
    src/chash.c clist.c mmapstring.c
                              the containers those parsers use
    include/libetpan/         only the headers the above actually include
    rp_charconv.c             OURS — see below

## What was deliberately dropped

**The write side.** `mailimf_write_*.c` and `mailmime_write_*.c` build
messages; we only ever read them. Their headers are still vendored because
`mailimf.h` includes them, but no writer object is compiled.

**`charconv.c`.** Upstream's charset layer is ~1100 lines of #ifdefs over
iconv, ICU and CoreFoundation, and with none of them configured it fails
*silently* — an RFC 2047 Subject comes back as the raw `=?utf-8?b?...?=`
with no error reported anywhere. rampart already has `rp_charset_to_utf8()`,
already links iconv, and already has a fallback for when iconv is missing.
`rp_charconv.c` implements the same three-function interface on top of it, so
the module has ONE charset stack rather than two that can disagree. It is
about 60 lines against upstream's 1100.

**`timeutils.c` and `mailimf_types_helper.c`.** The helper is for *building*
messages, not parsing them, and it is the only thing that pulls in
`timeutils.c` — which carries BSD **4-clause** code from the Regents of the
University of California (the advertising clause; rescinded by Berkeley in
1999, but still a licence we would rather not carry). Dropping the helper
drops the 4-clause code with it, and parsing is unaffected: verified by
building and running the parser without either file.

The remaining files were checked for the sendmail / NetBSD / Cyrus IMAP
attributions the upstream COPYRIGHT mentions. None appear.

## The HAVE_* defines are not optional

Upstream is autoconf'd, and guards a great deal on `HAVE_*` macros that
`configure` would have supplied. We build without `HAVE_CONFIG_H`, so those
macros are ours to provide — and when we do not, the guarded code silently
disappears rather than failing to compile. CMakeLists.txt supplies them.
Two of them bite in ways worth knowing:

**`HAVE_UNISTD_H`.** `mailmime_types_helper.c` calls `gethostname()` and
`getpid()`, with `<unistd.h>` behind that guard. Debian's headers pull
`unistd.h` in transitively, so a local build is perfectly happy; the
manylinux 2_28 oven's glibc 2.28 headers do not, and the build dies on
implicit declarations. **A local build passing proves nothing here** — this
one only appears in the oven.

**`HAVE_PTHREAD_H` + `LIBETPAN_REENTRANT`.** These are what make `MUTEX_LOCK`
in `mmapstring.c` an actual mutex instead of an empty macro. That lock guards
a **process-global** reference-count hashtable, and `mmap_string_unref()`
touches it for every decoded MIME part. Without both defines the lock is a
no-op, so two rampart server threads converting mail at the same time race on
a shared hash table. This does not fail to build, does not fail a test, and
does not show up until it corrupts a heap under load. If you ever find these
defines removed "because they weren't needed", put them back.

## Updating

This is a subset, so `git pull` is not the update path. Re-extract: take the
`.c` files listed in CMakeLists.txt from `src/low-level/imf`,
`src/low-level/mime` and `src/data-types`, recompute the header set from what
those files include, and keep `rp_charconv.c`. Do not vendor `charconv.c`,
`timeutils.c` or `mailimf_types_helper.c` without re-reading the two sections
above.
